     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-31 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Anantapur District Police Launch 'Ananta Kavach' App for Election Coordination</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Anantapur district police have introduced a mobile application named 'Ananta Kavach' to modernize and strengthen election security arrangements. Launched by Superintendent of Police P. Jagadeesh, the digital system integrates approximately 1,500 police personnel and all polling stations. The app tracks mapped polling stations, CCTV camera locations, and the live movements of deployed mobile parties at the Command Control Centre.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-31/anantapur-district-police-launch-ananta-kavach-app-for-elect.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The application allows field personnel to directly report law and order emergencies, triggering immediate alerts to the three nearest mobile parties for a rapid response. In addition to operational coordination, the platform accommodates personnel welfare by enabling staff to submit food requirements. SP Jagadeesh emphasized that the initiative ensures transparent, peaceful elections while prioritizing the needs of working police officers.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures all operational highlights and welfare features of the new 'Ananta Kavach' app accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/andhra-pradesh/app-launched-for-effective-coordination-during-election-bandobust/article71648398.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
