       <h1>Global Geopolitical Shifts: Iran Diplomacy, Israel Election, and Serbia Reform</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Regional powers, including Saudi Arabia, Türkiye, and Pakistan, met with Iranian leaders in Tehran on October 26 to discuss security, though no concrete breakthrough was reached. Simultaneously, Israel faces a hung parliament after a recent election, with Netanyahu’s bloc securing 53 seats and opposition forces winning 48, making coalition negotiations critical to forming a government.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-31/global-geopolitical-shifts-iran-diplomacy-israel-election-an.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In Serbia, a student-backed electoral list has ended the ruling party’s 14-year governance following two years of anti-corruption protests. The newly successful movement now faces the complex challenge of forming a government and delivering reforms, which may significantly influence the nation's future ties with Russia and the European Union.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a concise, accurate summary of all three geopolitical stories presented in the source text.</p>
