     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-31 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>South Africa's Historic 2-1 Series Win Over Australia Reshapes WTC 2025-27 Standings</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#WTC</span> <span>#South Africa</span> <span>#Australia</span></div>
       <p>South Africa secured a historic 2-1 Test series victory against Australia at Newlands, Cape Town, on October 31, 2026, winning the final match by 176 runs. This commanding home series win, captained by Temba Bavuma for the Proteas against Pat Cummins' Australians, significantly altered the ICC World Test Championship (WTC) 2025-27 points table. The result saw South Africa climb to second place, while Australia dropped from the top spot to third.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-31/south-africas-historic-2-1-series-win-over-australia-reshape.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>On the final day of the Cape Town Test, Australia required 288 runs with five wickets remaining, but their resistance was swiftly dismantled. Kagiso Rabada initiated the collapse with a devastating opening spell, claiming three crucial wickets by removing Matt Renshaw for a duck, Marnus Labuschagne for 4, and Travis Head for 14. Off-spinner Simon Harmer then took over, adding three more wickets in the first session, including Mitchell Starc for a 25-ball duck, to reduce Australia to 42 for 6 before lunch. Australia were eventually bowled out for just 111, handing South Africa the decisive 176-run victory.</p><p>This win boosted South Africa's WTC points percentage from 66.67 to 71.42. Australia, who had entered the match at the summit with a 75.00% percentage from 13 Tests (9 wins, 4 losses, 108 points), saw their percentage fall to 69.23, placing them third. New Zealand now hold the top position with 72.22% from 6 Tests (4 wins, 1 loss, 1 draw, 52 points). India sits fourth with 51.52% (68 points from 11 Tests, 5 wins, 4 losses, 2 draws), followed by Bangladesh at 47.61% (40 points from 7 Tests, 3 wins, 3 losses, 1 draw). England are sixth with 38.54% (74 points from 16 Tests, 7 wins, 8 losses, 1 draw). Sri Lanka and West Indies are at 33.33% and 26.92% respectively, while Pakistan languish at the bottom with 4.63%. Bangladesh also recently lost to West Indies by 8 wickets in the first Test at Mirpur, a result that will further impact their standing.</p><p>The Proteas' 2-1 series triumph was forged through hard-fought contests. Australia lost the opening match in Durban by 214 runs, followed by South Africa's victory in the second Test in Gqeberha by an innings, establishing a 2-0 lead. Australia managed to secure one win before the decider in Cape Town, where South Africa ultimately sealed both the series and their improved WTC standing. Rabada's incisive new-ball efforts and Harmer's controlled bowling in the middle overs were pivotal throughout the deciding Test.</p><p>What to watch: Upcoming series, including New Zealand vs India starting November 19 and Australia's tour of India early next year, could further reshape the WTC 2025-27 table.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, accurately incorporating all key figures, match details, and the context of the WTC points table provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
