     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-31 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Teenager Gunisha Kaur Creates Inclusive Pollinator Gardens, Earns Eco-Hero Award</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Youth Achievement</span> <span>#Pollinators</span> <span>#Inclusion</span></div>
       <p>Gunisha Kaur, a 16-year-old from Ludhiana, Punjab, has established over 20 pollinator gardens and ecological restoration projects through her initiative, Buzzing Blooms. These projects span Ludhiana, Meerut, Mumbai, and partner communities, transforming underused urban spaces into chemical-free habitats for bees, butterflies, and other pollinators.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-31/teenager-gunisha-kaur-creates-inclusive-pollinator-gardens-e.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Her impactful work recently earned her third place at the 2026 International Young Eco-Hero Awards, presented by Action For Nature. Over 24 months of monitoring, the gardens recorded a 155% increase in pollinator species richness, growing from 18 species to 46. Bee visits also saw a significant rise, increasing by nearly 200%.</p><p>Launched on March 5, 2023, Buzzing Blooms was inspired by Gunisha's concern over declining pollinator populations and her personal experience with her younger autistic brother. She observed how calming natural surroundings were for him and recognized the scarcity of outdoor spaces designed to accommodate neurodivergent children's sensory needs. The gardens feature native plants, structured pathways, and touch-safe greenery, making nature more accessible. Her Sikh heritage, which emphasizes respect for nature, also informs her approach to nurturing both biodiversity and belonging.</p><p>Beyond creating physical gardens, Buzzing Blooms has engaged more than 4,000 students, including over 250 neurodivergent and vulnerable individuals, reaching more than 8,000 people through workshops, restoration drives, and inclusive learning programs. Gunisha also established the Buzzing Blooms Peer Advocacy Network, training over 500 young ambassadors. To fund her efforts, she conducts art classes on weekends, reinvesting earnings alongside money from plant sales and scholarships. Additionally, Gunisha developed AeroBloom, an AI-guided aerial pollination system to support agriculture during extreme heat, earning her a bronze medal at the Indian Science and Engineering Fair.</p><p>Why it matters: This initiative demonstrates how youth-led projects can successfully integrate ecological restoration with social inclusion, creating tangible benefits for both nature and diverse communities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a comprehensive overview of Gunisha Kaur's work, including the statistics on pollinator growth, her motivation, and her technological innovations.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/changemakers/gunisha-kaur-pollinator-gardens-internation-eco-hero-award-ludhiana-india-12768795" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
