     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-31 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>TSA Rules: Power Banks and Tech Items That Raise Airport Security Red Flags</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Travelers packing electronics must adhere to specific TSA regulations to avoid security delays at airport checkpoints. Portable power banks, smartphones, tablets, laptops, and handheld gaming systems must be packed strictly in carry-on luggage and are prohibited in checked bags. Laptops and other electronics should be removed from bags during security scans.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-31/tsa-rules-power-banks-and-tech-items-that-raise-airport-secu.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Most portable power banks stay under the 100 watt-hour limit and are permitted in carry-on bags. However, lithium-ion batteries exceeding 100Wh require airline approval and are limited to two per person. Non-rechargeable lithium metal batteries are accepted up to 2 grams of lithium content. Additionally, electronic cigarettes and vapes must travel in carry-on luggage.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Breezy and precise summary of TSA rules for electronics, power banks, and vapes based entirely on source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2301241/tsa-electronics-red-flags/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
